Month = {JUN 11},
Abstract = {Reactive astrocytes evolve after brain injury, inflammatory and
   degenerative diseases, whereby they undergo transcriptomic
   re-programming. In malignant brain tumors, their function and crosstalk
   to other components of the environment is poorly understood. Here we
   report a distinct transcriptional phenotype of reactive astrocytes from
   glioblastoma linked to JAK/STAT pathway activation. Subsequently, we
   investigate the origin of astrocytic transformation by a microglia
   loss-of-function model in a human organotypic slice model with injected
   tumor cells. RNA-seq based gene expression analysis of astrocytes
   reveals a distinct astrocytic phenotype caused by the coexistence of
   microglia and astrocytes in the tumor environment, which leads to a
   large release of anti-inflammatory cytokines such as TGF beta, IL10 and
   G-CSF. Inhibition of the JAK/STAT pathway shifts the balance of pro- and
   anti-inflammatory cytokines towards a pro-inflammatory environment. The
   complex interaction of astrocytes and microglia cells promotes an
   immunosuppressive environment, suggesting that tumor-associated
   astrocytes contribute to anti-inflammatory responses.},
